<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#earl-of-derby">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Earl of Derby</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> is a title in the peerage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> held since 1485 by the Stanley family. Thomas Stanley received it from Henry VII after the Battle of Bosworth, and his heirs were also Lords of Mann until 1736. The 7th Earl, James Stanley, held the island for Charles I and was executed in 1651, and later earls included the Victorian prime minister Edward Smith-Stanley, 14th Earl.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby.jpg" alt="Earl of Derby" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Stanley,_Earls_of_Derby.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title is older than the Stanley line. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">King Stephen</a> created Robert de Ferrers the first Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> for his conduct at the Battle of Northallerton, and the Ferrers family held it until the last earl of the line, another Robert de Ferrers, rebelled against Henry III and forfeited his lands and earldom in 1266. Henry III gave the estates to his son Edmund Crouchback. In 1337 Henry of Grosmont, later Duke of Lancaster, was created Earl of Derby; the title passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-england">Henry Bolingbroke</a> and merged in the Crown when he became Henry IV in 1399.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Stanleys descended from Ligulf of Aldithley, and their name comes from Stoneley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a>. Thomas Stanley, 2nd Baron Stanley, the first Stanley earl, was the husband of Lady Margaret Beaufort, mother of Henry VII. The title may derive from the family's lands in the hundred of West Derby, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, rather than the county or city of Derby. The 3rd Earl was Lord High Steward at the coronation of Queen Mary in 1553, the 4th sat among the peers at the trial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots">Mary, Queen of Scots</a>, in 1586, and the 5th, Ferdinando, died in mysterious circumstances, some claiming he was poisoned.</p><p>James Stanley, 7th Earl, a staunch Royalist, moved to the Isle of Man in 1643 and made it a Royalist stronghold; his wife Charlotte de la Trémouille defended Lathom House in 1644. When the 10th Earl died childless in 1736, the earldom passed to a distant relative, Sir Edward Stanley, 5th Baronet, of Bickerstaffe. The 14th Earl, Edward Geoffrey Smith-Stanley, a noted parliamentary orator, began as a Whig, became a Tory and was three times Prime Minister. His son the 15th Earl served as Foreign Secretary and later joined the Liberal Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Several generations of the earls were prominent members of the Conservative Party, and at least one historian has compared the family's place in the party with that of the Cecils. The 12th Earl founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> horse race; the 16th Earl, Governor General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, gave the Stanley Cup in 1892; and the 17th Earl, ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, gave the Lord Derby Cup of French rugby league. The term local derby may also come from the title. The 13th Earl's zoological collections founded Liverpool Museum, and Edward Lear wrote The Owl and the Pussycat for his children. The family seat is Knowsley Hall, near Liverpool.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Earl of Derby” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Gascon campaign of the Hundred Years' War, an Anglo-Gascon force of 1,200 under Henry, <b>Earl of Derby</b>, heavily defeated a French army of 7,000 under Louis of Poitiers at Auberoche, near Périgueux.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-21">21 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
